        "page": 150,
        "chunk_id": "d3d33e53ea2478fe3729bba7019d8604095c5b6c2c2d3dc0bd7e9608a9931ebb:p150:c1",
        "score": 0.015625,
        "excerpt": "ty plot as a result of the increase in  \nφD  because of the presence of gas.  In uncompacted formations, the sonic  t  reading \nmay also be increased by the effect of the gas.\nHydrocarbon shifts in oil-bearing formations are usually negligible;  for clean formations, \nporosities can be read directly from the porosity graduations on the chart.\n11 M-N PLOT\nIn more complex mineral mixtures, lithology interpretation is facilitated by use of \nthe M-N plot.  These plots combine the data of all three porosity logs to provide \nthe lithology-dependent quantities M and N.  M and N are simply the slopes of the \nindividual lithology lines on the sonic-density and density neutron crossplot charts, \nrespectively.  Thus, M and N are essentially independent of porosity, and a crossplot \nprovides lithology identification."
